"""A reel that shows what bitwall does, saying so as it goes, on one wall,
over and over.

    demos/reel localhost:reel
    demos/reel --once --repo github.com/someone/bitwall pi.local:1
    demos/reel --scenes intro localhost:reel
    demos/reel --scenes intro.secure,panes,swap localhost:reel

It is a controller of its own: it starts the repo's senders on the wall,
and on panes of it, as each scene wants them, stops them when the scene
is over, and writes what the scene shows to a caption
(`demos/caption`) along the bottom of the wall. Six scenes: the intro (a
question typed, and a board that grows to private data the wall never
holds), the idea (a sender, the wire, a wall), more (characters, fonts,
panes, a layer, the terminal), pixels (a film, framed, text over it,
subtitles, the native receivers), running walls (the stats overlay, the
glass captured live), and building your own (a complete sender in Python
and in Rust, and a closer typed out). Every sender is one anyone with the repo has; the film is Tears
of Steel in `media/` (CC BY 3.0, the Blender Foundation,
mango.blender.org), the Rust sender `bitwall/rust/examples/wave.rs`,
built with cargo, and the control page is pictured by Chrome; a scene
whose part is missing is skipped.

The reel is scenes, chapters played in order, each a run of shots;
`--scenes` takes a scene's name for all its shots, or `scene.shot`, or a
shot's own name, for one.

A base sender takes the base from whoever holds it, so each shot starts
what it shows before it stops what it replaces, and the wall changes in
one move. A film's video base is the exception: it is stopped first, and
what follows takes the base it left. A sender is stopped with SIGTERM to
its process group and SIGKILL five seconds later, as a controller does.
Their logs are in `--logs`, a directory of its own each run.

The panes are walls on this machine: in its run directory for a wall on
this machine, and for a wall elsewhere in a run directory of the reel's
own, so their names meet nobody's. The control page it pictures is one
of its own too, on 127.0.0.1, shown those walls alone and given a home
of its own, so it knows of no other device and no sender a controller
started.

The reel draws its title cards, its diagrams and its code with a sender
of its own, this script given `--slides FILE`: a base sender whose slide
is the first line of FILE and its words the rest, read as it changes.
`--show` and `--pip` are its picture layers: an image over the whole
wall, and the wall's own glass, live, in a corner of itself.
"""
